---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe1505.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Applying to Every Set of ASPE Financial Statements" },
        { id: "s3", label: "What Counts as a \"Significant\" Accounting Policy" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Where Policy Disclosure Appears — the Initial Note" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Measurement Bases Used" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Focusing on Judgment and Choice, Not Boilerplate" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Industry-Specific and Entity-Specific Policies" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Disclosing a Genuinely Significant Judgment-Driven Policy" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Avoiding Boilerplate Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IAS 1's Accounting Policy Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">



<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Communicating the policies actually applied, clearly and concisely</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 1505 REQUIRES an ENTERPRISE to DISCLOSE its SIGNIFICANT accounting POLICIES — the MEASUREMENT bases USED and the SPECIFIC policies NECESSARY for a PROPER understanding of the FINANCIAL statements — PRESENTED in a CLEAR and CONCISE manner, TYPICALLY within the FIRST note to the FINANCIAL statements.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Applying to Every Set of ASPE Financial Statements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A universal disclosure requirement</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to EVERY complete SET of GENERAL-purpose FINANCIAL statements PREPARED in ACCORDANCE with ASPE, REGARDLESS of the ENTERPRISE'S size or INDUSTRY — accounting POLICY disclosure is a UNIVERSAL, foundational EXPECTATION.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>What Counts as a "Significant" Accounting Policy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Policies genuinely necessary for proper understanding</div><div class="text-supporting">A SIGNIFICANT accounting POLICY is ONE that is GENUINELY necessary for a PROPER understanding of the FINANCIAL statements — TYPICALLY, policies INVOLVING a CHOICE among ALTERNATIVES permitted BY GAAP, policies UNIQUE to the ENTERPRISE'S particular INDUSTRY, or policies INVOLVING SIGNIFICANT measurement JUDGMENT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Where Policy Disclosure Appears — the Initial Note</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A conventional, expected location</div><div class="text-supporting">SIGNIFICANT accounting POLICIES are NORMALLY disclosed WITHIN the FIRST note to the FINANCIAL statements — a CONVENTION that HELPS readers LOCATE this INFORMATION EFFICIENTLY, and PROVIDES essential CONTEXT before MOVING into the MORE detailed, TRANSACTION-specific notes THAT follow.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Measurement Bases Used</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Historical cost, fair value, or another basis, clearly identified</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE MORE than ONE measurement BASIS is USED within the FINANCIAL statements (e.g. HISTORICAL cost FOR most items, FAIR value FOR certain financial INSTRUMENTS UNDER Section 3856), the ENTERPRISE clearly IDENTIFIES which BASIS applies to WHICH categories of ASSETS and LIABILITIES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Focusing on Judgment and Choice, Not Boilerplate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Meaningful disclosure, not a mechanical recitation of every possible policy</div><div class="text-supporting">Section 1505 EXPLICITLY emphasizes that POLICY disclosure should FOCUS on policies GENUINELY unique TO the entity OR industry, or THOSE involving SIGNIFICANT judgment OR a choice AMONG alternatives PERMITTED by GAAP — RATHER than a MECHANICAL, boilerplate RECITATION of EVERY conceivable accounting POLICY an enterprise MIGHT apply. THIS is a GENUINELY practical, focused APPROACH, AVOIDING disclosure OVERLOAD that PROVIDES limited GENUINE value to READERS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Industry-Specific and Entity-Specific Policies</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Where an enterprise's particular circumstances warrant extra explanation</div><div class="text-supporting">POLICIES that are PARTICULAR to a SPECIFIC industry (e.g. LONG-term construction CONTRACTS, agricultural PRODUCTION) or UNIQUE to an ENTERPRISE'S own CIRCUMSTANCES (e.g. an UNUSUAL revenue ARRANGEMENT) WARRANT EXPLICIT disclosure, GIVEN their LIKELY significance TO a reader'S UNDERSTANDING.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Disclosing a Genuinely Significant Judgment-Driven Policy</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A policy genuinely worth explaining</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An income tax accounting policy choice</div><div class="wk-body">A private enterprise elects to apply the Taxes Payable Method (rather than the Future Income Taxes Method) for its income tax accounting under Section 3465.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY discloses THIS policy CHOICE clearly WITHIN its INITIAL accounting POLICIES note — GIVEN it REPRESENTS a GENUINE choice AMONG two PERMITTED alternatives UNDER ASPE, and MATERIALLY affects HOW readers should INTERPRET the ABSENCE of any DEFERRED tax balances ELSEWHERE in the FINANCIAL statements.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Avoiding Boilerplate Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Skipping a policy disclosure that adds no genuine value</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A routine, non-judgmental cash policy</div><div class="wk-body">A private enterprise holds ordinary operating bank account balances, with no unusual cash management arrangements, foreign currency holdings, or cash equivalent classification judgments involved.</div><div class="wk-answer">GIVEN the ROUTINE, non-JUDGMENTAL nature of this BALANCE, the COMPANY need NOT include an EXTENSIVE, boilerplate "CASH accounting POLICY" note — CONSISTENT with Section 1505'S focus ON genuinely SIGNIFICANT policies, RATHER than MECHANICAL completeness.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 1505 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Measurement bases used in preparing the financial statements</span><span>Accounting policies genuinely necessary for a proper understanding of the financial statements</span><span>Industry-specific or entity-specific policies, and policies involving significant judgment or choice among alternatives</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Which policies are genuinely significant enough to warrant explicit disclosure, versus routine and self-evident</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal — this Section is a disclosure standard, not a measurement standard</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 1's Accounting Policy Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 1505 vs IAS 1</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 1505</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 1</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Focus on materiality/significance</span><span>Explicit — avoids boilerplate</span><span>Similar — IAS 1 (post-2018 materiality amendments) also discourages boilerplate</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">BROADLY aligned in SPIRIT — BOTH frameworks HAVE moved TOWARD encouraging FOCUSED, meaningful POLICY disclosure OVER exhaustive, BOILERPLATE recitation.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where this Section's requirements apply</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Concept</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Accounting policy disclosures</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No effect on primary financial statement figures</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1506</span><span>Accounting Changes — governs how a change to a previously disclosed policy is itself accounted for and disclosed</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1508</span><span>Measurement Uncertainty — supplements this Section for disclosure of estimation uncertainty specifically</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Significant accounting policy</div><div class="glossary-def">A policy genuinely necessary for a proper understanding of the financial statements, typically involving a choice among GAAP-permitted alternatives or significant judgment.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Boilerplate disclosure</div><div class="glossary-def">Generic, mechanically-recited policy language that provides limited genuine value to readers, which Section 1505 discourages in favor of focused, meaningful disclosure.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe1505 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
